<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19868691</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2128384</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>DIFFERENCES BETWEEN MITOCHONDRIA AND BACTERIA</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Cowdry</surname><given-names>Edmund V.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Olitsky</surname><given-names>Peter K.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1c595b0N0x38f1f58">From the Laboratories of The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>31</day><month>10</month><year>1922</year></pub-date><volume>36</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>521</fpage><lpage>533</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>9</day><month>6</month><year>1922</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1922, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> A direct comparison of mitochondria and bacteria in the living condition, as well as in permanent preparations, subjected to the same influences, and viewed side by side, reveals microchemical and tinctorial differences which can only be attributed to a fundamental dissimilarity in their chemical constitution. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>